 @PathVariable int id

This grabs the id directly from the URL. If the user goes to /product/5, this parameter captures the number 5 so your code knows exactly which product to update in the database.

@RequestPart

When you submit a form that contains both regular text data (like a product's name, price, or description) AND a file (like a .jpg or .png), you cannot send it as a simple JSON package.
You have to send it as something called multipart/form-data.

Search Bar

To create a search bar in spring boot use a 'keyword', variable manage it, and create a special query in the repository interface to use that 'keyword' variable to access
    the List of Products.
Use JPQL query language to write the query in the Repository.
first Create the Draft of the query use '@Query' annotation, then controller, then service, then the query it self.
The query is " @Query("SELECT p FROM Product p WHERE" +
              "LOWER(p.name) LIKE LOWER(CONCAT('%' :keyword '%')) OR" +
              LOWER(p.brand) LIKE LOWER(CONCAT('%' :keyword '%'))" "

It defines a custom database query to search for products by name,brand etc... making the search case-insensitive and matching partial words.
:keyword: The colon (:) denotes a named parameter. This is a placeholder that will be replaced by the actual string variable passed into your repository method.

CONCAT('%', :keyword, '%'): The CONCAT function glues strings together. The % symbol is a wildcard that means "any characters can go here."
By wrapping your keyword in percents (%keyword%), you are telling the database: "Find any product name where the keyword appears anywhere inside it.

